201 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 114 0 R/E(A Sign of Spring in the Village)/Pg 12 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

202 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 114 0 R/E(If you\220ve taken a walk through the neighborhood lately, you\220ve likely been stopped in your tracks by a burst of giant, goblet-shaped flowers. That stunning display belongs to the Saucer Magnolia. Often called "tulip trees" by neighbors, these local favorites are one of the earliest signs that spring has arrived in Lakewood.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

203 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 540 0 R/E(Cool Factor: Magnolias are ancient! They evolved before bees even existed, so they are biologically designed to be pollinated by beetles.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

204 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 114 0 R/E(Neighbor Spotlight: Mark\220s Backyard Feeder)/Pg 12 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

205 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 114 0 R/E(It\220s not just the plants waking up; our local bird population is busy too! Our neighbor Mark shared some wonderful "action shots" from his bird feeder last month.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

206 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 114 0 R/E(Dark-eyed Junco: Easily spotted by its crisp black "hood" and white belly.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

207 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 541 0 R/E(Cool Factor: They are known as "Snowbirds" because their arrival often signals the start of colder weather \(or in our case, the peak of our "California Winter"\).)/Pg 12 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

208 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 114 0 R/E(Mourning Dove: A gentle presence in the Village, known for its soft cooing and plump feathers.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

209 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 542 0 R/E(Cool Factor: These birds can fly up to 55 miles per hour! They may look sleepy on a fence, but they are incredibly fast fliers.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

210 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 114 0 R/E(White-crowned Sparrow: Showing off its distinctive black-and-white striped head.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[19]>>
endobj

211 0 obj
